sections.s2_technical:
An IAM User represents one identity with durable credentials. You give Rajesh a User with a console password for the web UI and optionally an access key ID and secret for the CLI or SDK. The problem is that those access keys are long-lived — if they leak, an attacker can use them until someone notices. That is why we avoid Users for applications.

An IAM Group exists purely for convenience. You never log in "as a group." You create a group called `Developers`, attach the policies once, and add Users to it. When a new engineer joins, you add them to the group and they instantly inherit the right permissions. Remove them from the group and the permissions disappear. A User can belong to multiple groups.

An IAM Role is the modern, secure way to grant permissions. A Role has no password and no permanent access keys. Instead, a trusted principal "assumes" the Role through STS (Security Token Service) and receives temporary credentials that expire, typically in an hour. An EC2 instance, a Lambda function, or a user from another account can assume a Role. The Role has two policies: a trust policy saying who is allowed to assume it, and permission policies saying what it can do once assumed.

```java
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.sts.StsClient;
import software.amazon.awssdk.services.sts.model.AssumeRoleRequest;

StsClient sts = StsClient.create();
AssumeRoleRequest req = AssumeRoleRequest.builder()
        .roleArn("arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/StatementReaderRole")
        .roleSessionName("rajesh-session")
        .build();
// Returns temporary credentials that expire — far safer than embedded keys.
var creds = sts.assumeRole(req).credentials();
```

An IAM Policy is the JSON that actually decides allow or deny. It is attached to a User, Group, or Role. A minimal policy that lets the holder read one S3 bucket of statements looks like this:

```json
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [{
    "Effect": "Allow",
    "Action": ["s3:GetObject"],
    "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::nbi-customer-statements/*"
  }]
}
```

A senior nuance: permissions are the union of everything attached. If Rajesh is a User in the `Tellers` group with one policy and also has an inline policy of his own, his effective permissions are all of them combined, minus any explicit deny, which always wins.

sections.s4d_java:
```java
// ─── File: IamDemo.java ───
package com.bank.iam;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.logging.*;

public class IamDemo {
    private static final Logger log = Logger.getLogger("IAM");
    static {
        Handler h = new ConsoleHandler();
        h.setFormatter(new BankLogFormatter());
        log.setUseParentHandlers(false);
        log.addHandler(h);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        IamPolicy readStatements = new IamPolicy("ReadStatements", "ALLOW",
                "s3:GetObject", "arn:aws:s3:::nbi-customer-statements/*");

        IamGroup developers = new IamGroup("Developers");
        developers.attach(readStatements);

        IamUser rajesh = new IamUser("rajesh");
        rajesh.joinGroup(developers);              // inherits group policy

        IamRole serviceRole = new IamRole("StatementReaderRole");
        serviceRole.attach(readStatements);         // assumed by EC2, no keys

        AccessEvaluator evaluator = new AccessEvaluator();
        boolean userCan = evaluator.isAllowed(rajesh.effectivePolicies(),
                "s3:GetObject", "arn:aws:s3:::nbi-customer-statements/stmt1.pdf");
        boolean roleCan = evaluator.isAllowed(serviceRole.policies(),
                "s3:GetObject", "arn:aws:s3:::nbi-customer-statements/stmt1.pdf");

        log.info(() -> "User rajesh (via group) can read statement: " + userCan);
        log.info(() -> "Service via assumed Role can read statement: " + roleCan);
    }
}

// ─── Would be in IamPolicy.java ───
class IamPolicy {
    private final String name, effect, action, resource;
    IamPolicy(String name, String effect, String action, String resource) {
        this.name = name; this.effect = effect; this.action = action; this.resource = resource;
    }
    boolean matches(String act, String res) {
        boolean resMatch = res.matches(resource.replace("*", ".*"));
        return action.equals(act) && resMatch;
    }
    String effect() { return effect; }
}

// ─── Would be in IamGroup.java ───
class IamGroup {
    private final String name;
    private final List<IamPolicy> policies = new ArrayList<>();
    IamGroup(String name) { this.name = name; }
    void attach(IamPolicy p) { policies.add(p); }
    List<IamPolicy> policies() { return policies; }
}

// ─── Would be in IamUser.java ───
class IamUser {
    private final String name;
    private final List<IamGroup> groups = new ArrayList<>();
    private final List<IamPolicy> inline = new ArrayList<>();
    IamUser(String name) { this.name = name; }
    void joinGroup(IamGroup g) { groups.add(g); }
    List<IamPolicy> effectivePolicies() {
        List<IamPolicy> all = new ArrayList<>(inline);
        groups.forEach(g -> all.addAll(g.policies()));  // union of group + inline
        return all;
    }
}

// ─── Would be in IamRole.java ───
class IamRole {
    private final String name;
    private final List<IamPolicy> policies = new ArrayList<>();
    IamRole(String name) { this.name = name; }
    void attach(IamPolicy p) { policies.add(p); }
    List<IamPolicy> policies() { return policies; }
}

// ─── Would be in AccessEvaluator.java ───
class AccessEvaluator {
    boolean isAllowed(List<IamPolicy> policies, String action, String resource) {
        boolean allow = false;
        for (IamPolicy p : policies) {
            if (p.matches(action, resource)) {
                if ("DENY".equals(p.effect())) return false;  // explicit deny wins
                if ("ALLOW".equals(p.effect())) allow = true;
            }
        }
        return allow;  // default implicit deny
    }
}

// ─── Would be in BankLogFormatter.java ───
class BankLogFormatter extends Formatter {
    @Override public String format(LogRecord r) {
        return String.format("%1$tF %1$tT [%2$s] %3$s%n", r.getMillis(), r.getLevel(), r.getMessage());
    }
}
